  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose a Novo Alternative",
    "body": "Choosing a Novo alternative comes down to five published numbers: interest, account count, branch access, currency fees and deposit insurance. Each one maps to a different provider on this list.\n\n- Interest on balances. Novo Business Checking pays no interest, and Novo Savings pays 2% APY only at $50,000 or more. Bluevine Standard pays 1.3% APY on checking up to $250,000 when the activity goal is met, and Relay Starter pays 1.19% APY on savings at $0 per month.\n- Number of accounts. Relay Starter includes 20 checking accounts per business, and Relay Scale includes 50.\n- Branches and cash. Chase lists 5,000 branches and more than 14,000 ATMs, and Business Complete Banking includes $5,000 in in-branch cash deposits each statement cycle.\n- Currencies. Wise Business gives account details to receive money in 22 currencies, with conversion fees from 0.23%.\n- Deposit insurance. Novo and Mercury list up to $5M. Relay, Bluevine and Lili list up to $3M. Wise lists pass-through FDIC insurance up to $250K on interest-earning funds only.\n\nPlan fees change the math. A paid tier with a higher APY only beats a free tier when the extra interest on the actual balance is larger than the yearly plan fee, and each section below shows that arithmetic with the published numbers.\n\nLending is the last filter. Bluevine lists a Line of Credit up to $250K, a Term Loan up to $500K from lending partners and SBA 7(a) loans up to $350K. Lili lists up to $250,000 in flexible capital embedded in the account, and Novo lists business credit, business loans and a business credit card under its Products menu."
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "Novo vs Mercury on Wires and Treasury Yield",
      "body": "Mercury is a fintech that offers a $0 plan plus two paid tiers. It is not an FDIC-insured bank. Banking services come through Choice Financial Group and Column N.A., Members FDIC, and Mercury lists up to $5M in FDIC insurance, the same ceiling Novo lists.\n\nThe Mercury plan costs $0 per month with no monthly fees, overdraft penalties or minimum balances. It includes free ACH (same-day and next-day), free domestic wires and real-time payments in USD. USD international wires cost $0, while non-USD international wires carry a 1% currency exchange fee, so a business sending $10,000 in a foreign currency pays $100 in exchange fees.\n\nMercury Plus costs $29.90 per month on annual pricing, which is $29.90 x 12 = $358.80 per year, with a 30-day free trial. Plus adds invoicing with ACH debit at $1 per transaction. Mercury Pro costs $299 per month on annual pricing, or $3,588 per year, and drops the ACH debit invoice charge to $0 per transaction.\n\nMercury Books costs $35 per month and Mercury Payroll costs $100 per month plus $50 per person paid, and both add-ons are waived until Dec 31, 2026. Novo includes payroll, bookkeeping tools and free invoices with automatic reminders in its one account at $0 per month.\n\nMercury Treasury lists a yield up to 4.42% for eligible customers with a $250K Mercury balance. Mercury Advisory, LLC offers it as a Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) registered investment adviser, and the yield is subject to investment risk. Mercury also lists corporate credit cards with 1.5% cashback and a free Mercury Personal account with any business plan.\n\nMercury fits businesses that send USD international wires or keep a balance of $250K or more. Novo fits businesses that want payroll and bookkeeping tools without an add-on fee after Dec 31, 2026."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Relay Checking Accounts and Tiered Savings Rates",
      "body": "Relay is a fintech that sells three plans built around multiple checking accounts and tiered savings rates. It is not an FDIC-insured bank. Banking services come from Thread Bank, Member FDIC, and deposits qualify for up to $3,000,000 in FDIC coverage through a sweep program.\n\nRelay Starter costs $0 per month and includes 1.19% APY on savings, 1% cash back on credit cards, 20 checking accounts per business and built-in spend controls. Relay Grow costs $30 per month, or $360 per year, with a 14-day trial. Grow raises savings to 1.87% APY and cash back to 1.25%, and adds multi-step approval rules, recurring invoices and bookkeeping automations.\n\nRelay Scale costs $90 per month on a limited-time discount from $120, which is $1,080 per year. Scale pays 3.21% APY, 1.5% cash back, 50 checking accounts per business and no fee for same-day ACH. Every plan includes accounting integrations and unlimited users, and Relay notes that additional transaction fees apply.\n\nThe interest math depends on the balance. A business holding $100,000 for a full year on Scale earns $100,000 x 3.21% = $3,210, minus $1,080 in plan fees, for $2,130 net. The same $100,000 on Starter earns $1,190 with no plan fee. Novo Savings at $50,000 earns $50,000 x 2% = $1,000.\n\nRelay states that its rates are accurate as of the date on its pricing page and may change. Relay fits businesses that separate money into many checking accounts for taxes, payroll or projects. Novo fits businesses that need one checking account with ATM fee reimbursement, which Relay does not list."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Bluevine Checking Interest and Business Lending",
      "body": "Bluevine is a fintech that pays interest on business checking and offers loans through its own lending products. It is not a bank. Deposits are FDIC-insured through Coastal Community Bank, Member FDIC, up to $3 million per depositor via multiple banks.\n\nBluevine Standard has no monthly fee, no overdraft fees, no minimum balance, free standard ACH, unlimited transactions and no incoming domestic wire or ACH fees. It pays 1.3% APY on balances up to $250,000 when the monthly activity goal is met. That goal is either $500 per month spent with the Bluevine Business Debit Mastercard or $2,500 per month received or deposited in customer payments.\n\nBluevine Plus costs $30 per month, or $360 per year, and pays 1.75% APY on balances up to $250,000 with no activity requirement. Premier costs $95 per month, or $1,140 per year, and pays 3.3% APY on all balances with no activity requirement. Bluevine lists conditions under which these fees can be waived.\n\nOn $100,000 held for a year, Premier earns $3,300 against $1,140 in plan fees, for $2,160 net. Standard earns $100,000 x 1.3% = $1,300 with no plan fee when the activity goal is met. Novo Business Checking earns $0 on any balance.\n\nBluevine lists a Line of Credit up to $250K, a Term Loan up to $500K from lending partners with repayment terms up to 24 months and SBA 7(a) loans up to $350K. It also lists free invoicing, Tap to Pay and bill pay by same-day ACH, check, or domestic or international wire. Bluevine fits businesses that want interest on checking and a lender inside the same account."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Lili Savings Tiers and Embedded Capital",
      "body": "Lili is a fintech that pairs a $0 checking account with a tiered savings rate. It is not a bank. Banking services come from Sunrise Banks, N.A., Member FDIC, with FDIC insurance up to $3M through a network of sweep program banks.\n\nLili Core has $0 monthly fees, no minimum balance and a Lili Visa debit card. It charges no debit card fees for foreign transactions, in-network ATM use or inactivity. Core supports Express ACH, checks, and domestic and international wires, with fees on wires.\n\nInternational wires are only available to legal entities such as limited liability companies (LLCs), corporations and partnerships. Lili Pro, Smart and Premium are paid tiers, and lili.co points to Lili's fee schedule instead of showing the amounts. Cash back is limited to Pro, Smart and Premium, and accounting, tax preparation and invoicing software is limited to Smart and Premium.\n\nLili Savings is available on all Lili plans, with a variable APY effective January 13, 2026. It pays 2.25% on balances up to and including $500,000 and 4.00% on balances over $500,000 up to and including $1,000,000. Balances above $1,000,000 earn nothing.\n\nA business holding $100,000 in Lili Savings for a year earns $100,000 x 2.25% = $2,250. Novo Savings pays nothing below $50,000, while Lili lists no minimum balance for its savings rate. Lili also lists up to $250,000 in flexible capital embedded in the account.\n\nLili fits businesses that want savings interest from the first dollar. Novo fits businesses that want debit card cash back and invoicing on a $0 account, since Lili limits both to paid tiers."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Wise Business Multi-Currency Accounts and Transfer Fees",
      "body": "Wise Business is a multi-currency account that charges per transfer instead of a monthly subscription. Wise is a Money Service Business registered with the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN). It is not an FDIC-insured bank.\n\nRegistering and holding money are free. A one-time 31 USD set-up fee unlocks account details to receive money in 22 currencies. Sending fees start from 0.23% and converting money starts from 0.23%, both varying by currency.\n\nWise lists outgoing wire transfers from as low as $1.13. Receiving a domestic wire costs a fixed $6.11, and Wise's own comparison says US banks often charge $15 to $30 to receive. A business receiving ten domestic wires in a year pays 10 x $6.11 = $61.10 at Wise, while Novo accepts incoming domestic and international wires at no cost.\n\nThe Wise Multi-Currency Card has no Wise fee on ATM withdrawals up to 250 USD per calendar month. After that, each withdrawal costs 1.95 USD + 1.95%, so a 100 USD withdrawal past the allowance costs 1.95 + 1.95 = 3.90 USD, and ATM operators may add their own fees. Novo charges no ATM fees and reimburses third-party ATM fees up to $7 per month.\n\nWise lists a 3.68% variable APY on USD balances, which is $3,680 on $100,000 held for a year. Pass-through FDIC insurance covers up to $250K on interest-earning funds through program banks. Wise Business fits businesses that get paid in several currencies and convert between them."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Chase Business Complete Banking Branches and Monthly Fees",
      "body": "Chase Business Complete Banking is a branch bank checking account with a $15 monthly service fee. Chase lists access to more than 14,000 ATMs and 5,000 branches, which none of the fintech accounts on this list offer.\n\nThe $15 fee can be waived five ways, including a $2,000 minimum daily ending balance. Without a waiver, the fee totals $15 x 12 = $180 per year. Each statement cycle includes up to 20 paper and teller transactions and $5,000 in in-branch cash deposits, and Chase notes that fees may apply to wire transfers.\n\nChase Performance Business Checking lists at $40 or $0 with a waivable fee, which is $480 per year if the fee is not waived. It includes up to 250 account debits and $20,000 in in-branch cash deposits per statement cycle. Chase Platinum Business Checking lists at $95 or $0, which is $1,140 per year without a waiver, and includes up to 500 account debits and $25,000 in in-branch cash deposits.\n\nNovo has no monthly service fee and no minimum balance, so a business that cannot keep $2,000 at day's end pays $180 more per year at Chase. Novo's checking page lists no in-branch cash deposit allowance.\n\nChase Business Complete Banking fits businesses that handle cash in person and want a branch nearby. Novo fits businesses that bank fully online and want the $0 fee without tracking a daily balance."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who This Is Not For",
      "body": "Novo and each alternative leave out some businesses, and each gap has a listed fix.\n\n- Novo fits poorly for businesses that want interest on checking. Novo Business Checking pays nothing and Novo Savings pays only at $50,000 or more. Bluevine Standard pays 1.3% APY on checking up to $250,000 when the activity goal is met, at $0 per month.\n- Novo fits poorly for heavy ATM users, because reimbursement stops at $7 per month. A business paying $3 in third-party ATM fees three times in one month pays $9 and gets $7 back. Chase lists more than 14,000 ATMs for that pattern.\n- Mercury Treasury fits poorly for businesses below a $250K Mercury balance, since that is the eligibility line. Relay Starter and Lili Savings pay APY on smaller balances.\n- Relay Scale and Bluevine Premier fit poorly for smaller balances. At $30,000, Premier earns $30,000 x 3.3% = $990 against $1,140 in fees, and Scale earns $963 against $1,080. Bluevine Standard and Relay Starter charge $0 per month.\n- Wise Business fits poorly for businesses that want FDIC coverage on every dollar, since pass-through insurance covers only interest-earning funds up to $250K. Novo, Mercury, Relay, Bluevine and Lili list sweep coverage from $3M to $5M.\n- Lili fits poorly for businesses that need international wires but are not legal entities such as LLCs, corporations or partnerships. Novo and Bluevine both list international wires without that restriction on their pages.\n- Chase Business Complete Banking fits poorly for businesses that cannot meet a waiver and want to avoid $180 per year. Novo, Mercury, Relay, Bluevine and Lili all list a $0 checking option."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Would Change Our Answer on Novo Alternatives",
      "body": "Several published changes would shift which Novo alternative fits which business:\n\n- Novo adding interest to Business Checking, or lowering the $50,000 balance needed for 2% APY on Novo Savings.\n- Novo raising or removing the $7 monthly cap on third-party ATM fee reimbursement.\n- Bluevine changing its activity goal of $500 in debit card spending or $2,500 in customer payments for the 1.3% APY.\n- Relay ending the limited-time $90 Scale price and returning to $120 per month, which is $1,440 per year instead of $1,080.\n- Mercury ending the waiver on Mercury Books and Mercury Payroll after Dec 31, 2026. Mercury Books alone would cost $35 x 12 = $420 per year.\n- Wise changing its $6.11 domestic wire receiving fee or its 31 USD set-up fee.\n- Lili publishing Pro, Smart and Premium prices on lili.co, which would allow a direct cost comparison with Novo's $0 account."
    }
  ],
  "stillBetterFit": {
    "heading": "When Novo Is Still the Better Fit",
    "body": "Novo fits businesses that want $0 checking with banking, invoicing and bill pay in one account. Novo Business Checking has no monthly service fee, no fee to open, no minimum balance and no required minimum or maximum deposit.\n\nThe yearly cost gap is plain arithmetic. Novo costs $0 per year, compared with $358.80 for Mercury Plus, $360 for Relay Grow, $360 for Bluevine Plus and $180 for Chase Business Complete Banking without a waiver. Novo also accepts incoming domestic and international wires at no cost, while Wise charges $6.11 per incoming domestic wire.\n\nNovo's one account covers payments, free invoices with automatic reminders, card and link payments, bill pay, expense management, payroll and bookkeeping tools. Debit card cash back is 2% on eligible purchases with a checking balance of $5,000 or more and 1% below $5,000. FDIC insurance reaches $5,000,000 per depositor through Middlesex Federal Savings, F.A. and its sweep network, which is higher than the $3M Relay, Bluevine and Lili list.\n\nNovo's FAQ notes that transaction limits apply to ACH transfers, check deposits, ATM withdrawals and debit card purchases. Some of those limits can be raised after opening by request in the app or on the web, and Novo says an account takes about 10 minutes to open."
  },
  "verdict": "Novo fits businesses that want a $0 checking account with free standard ACH, free incoming wires, ATM fee reimbursement up to $7 per month and built-in invoicing and payroll. Mercury fits businesses that send USD international wires or hold $250K for Treasury yield. Relay fits businesses that need 20 to 50 checking accounts. Bluevine fits businesses that want interest on checking and access to lines of credit, term loans and SBA 7(a) loans. Lili fits businesses that want savings interest from the first dollar, Wise Business fits businesses that work in several currencies, and Chase Business Complete Banking fits businesses that deposit cash at a branch.\n\nFor a Novo alternative, the concrete next step is to write down the average monthly balance, the monthly count of ATM withdrawals and incoming wires, and any cash deposits, then read the matching row in the table above.",
